+## TL;DR
+- `LexNLP`, `ContraxSuite`, and `OpenNyAI` are the strongest legal-specific repositories in this space.
+- `Unstructured`, `Docling`, `PaddleOCR`, and `OCRmyPDF` are practical choices for messy legal document ingestion.
+- `Haystack`, `LangChain`, `LlamaIndex`, `Qdrant`, `Milvus`, and `Weaviate` cover most open-source legal RAG needs.
+- `Ollama`, `vLLM`, `TGI`, and `LocalAI` are the main self-hosted options for serving models inside a controlled environment.
+- `Whisper`, `Label Studio`, `doccano`, `n8n`, and `Flowise` help with deposition transcription, review pipelines, and workflow automation.
+
+## Why This List
+Open source matters for legal work because lawyers, law firms, legal ops teams, and compliance groups often need tighter control over confidentiality, auditability, deployment, retention, and vendor risk than closed SaaS products allow. A realistic list of the best AI tools for lawyers 2026 should therefore prioritize public GitHub repositories that can be inspected, self-hosted, extended, and combined into legal workflows instead of relying on proprietary black boxes.
