+# Contributing
+
+Thank you for contributing to `awesome-best-ai-tools-for-lawyers-2026`.
+
+## Submission Rules
+
+Every submission must satisfy all of the following:
+
+1. The tool must have a public `github.com` repository URL.
+2. The project must be open source.
+3. The tool must be relevant to lawyers, legal ops, compliance, e-discovery, contract work, legal research, document review, or closely related legal workflows.
+4. The submission must not be a proprietary SaaS product without a public GitHub repository.
+5. The pull request must place the repo in the best-fit existing category, or explain why a new category is needed.
+
+## What to Include
+
+When opening a pull request, include:
+
+- Repository name
+- Public GitHub URL
+- One short description based on the project README
+- Why it is useful for legal workflows
+- License information if visible on GitHub
+
+## Quality Bar
+
+Please avoid:
+
+- Closed-source products
+- Marketing links instead of GitHub repo links
+- Duplicate entries
+- Weakly related general tools with no plausible legal workflow use
+
+If a category has fewer than 5 strong GitHub repositories, keep that gap explicit instead of padding the list with poor fits.
+
+## Format
+
+Use this item structure in `README.md`:
+
+```md
+#### [Tool Name](https://github.com/username/repo)
+> **Description:** Short summary based on the GitHub README.
+
+- **GitHub:** [github.com/username/repo](https://github.com/username/repo)
+- **Stars:** GitHub-backed metadata
+- **Language:** GitHub-backed metadata
+- **License:** GitHub-backed metadata
+- **Last Commit:** GitHub-backed metadata
+- **Category:** tag1, tag2
+- **Best for:** specific legal use case
+```
+
+## Pull Requests
+
+- Keep pull requests focused.
+- One new tool or one coherent category update per pull request is preferred.
+- If metadata cannot be verified locally, explain what was checked and what still needs refresh.
+
+## Code of Conduct
+
+Be respectful, factual, and concise in review discussions.
